-- title:
--   Cyclic C-sets are determined by elementwise fixed-point counts
-- statement:
--   Let $C$ be a finite cyclic group, and let $X$ and $Y$ be finite sets carrying actions of $C$. The hypothesis is that for every $c \in C$ the fixed-point sets $X^{c} = \{x \in X : c \cdot x = x\}$ and $Y^{c}$ have the same cardinality, the cardinalities being taken as natural numbers via `Nat.card`. The conclusion is that there exists a bijection $e : X \simeq Y$ of the underlying sets which is $C$-equivariant, in the sense that $e(c \cdot x) = c \cdot e(x)$ for all $c \in C$ and all $x \in X$; equivariance is stated pointwise rather than by producing an isomorphism in a category of $C$-sets, and the bijection is merely asserted to exist.
--
--   This is Burnside's theorem on the table of marks, specialised to a cyclic group: since every subgroup of a finite cyclic group is generated by a single element, equality of the marks $|X^{F}|$ over all subgroups $F$ reduces to equality of the fixed-point counts $|X^{c}|$ over all group elements, so these counts determine a finite $C$-set up to equivariant bijection (cyclicity is essential; the Klein four-group admits counterexamples). It is used in the treatment of restricted permutation representations entering [`Rep.eq_zero_of_additive_of_forall_ind_isCyclic_coprime`](thm.html#Rep.eq_zero_of_additive_of_forall_ind_isCyclic_coprime), where a counting identity for fixed points is upgraded to an isomorphism of permutation modules.
-- source:
